[THOUGHT] The diagnosis of drug-induced pemphigus vulgaris (captopril-associated) is strongly supported by:  
- Classic clinical features (acute-onset flaccid blisters, positive Nikolsky sign);  
- Histopathologic hallmark (suprabasal acantholysis with intraepidermal blistering);  
- Immunopathologic confirmation (intercellular IgG and C3 on DIF — the serologic “gold standard” for PV);  
- Strict temporal association with captopril initiation and absence of alternative etiologies (no other drugs, no autoimmune comorbidities, no mucosal disease to suggest idiopathic PV);  
- Exclusion of mimics (e.g., bullous pemphigoid — subepidermal blistering, linear BMZ IgG; paraneoplastic pemphigus — multiorgan involvement, distinct autoantibody profile; IgA pemphigus — granular IgA on DIF).  

All eight evidence domains referenced (clinical, histopathologic, immunopathologic, temporal, pharmacologic, exclusionary, therapeutic response logic, and guideline concordance) are satisfied per current AAD/EDF diagnostic criteria for drug-induced pemphigus. No red-flag omissions: mucosal assessment is explicitly documented as negative (critical for distinguishing drug-induced from idiopathic PV), and systemic symptoms are absent — consistent with early, localized, drug-triggered presentation. While serum anti-Dsg3 antibodies would be supportive, they are *not required* for diagnosis when DIF is unequivocally positive and clinical/histologic correlation is perfect — especially in drug-induced cases where antibody titers may be low or transient. Turn limit is not yet reached (11/16), but data sufficiency is already met.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

